  <section class="section container prose">
    <span class="tag">Markets · Saudi Arabia</span>
    <h1>Truck batteries for the Saudi Arabia market</h1>
    <p class="byline">Published by Dingwei Battery · Last reviewed 2026-09-23 · Sources: SASO/SABER, GSO, National Center for Meteorology, Mordor Intelligence</p>
    <p class="lead">Saudi Arabia is a high-volume, high-heat commercial-vehicle market where Japanese and European heavy trucks run side by side. That means JIS N150/N200/N100 batteries for Hino, Isuzu, Fuso and UD Trucks, and DIN/EN codes alongside them for European fleets — all specified for summer temperatures that routinely push past 40&deg;C.</p>

    <DirectAnswer>Saudi Arabia's truck fleet is a mix of Japanese heavy trucks (Hino, Isuzu, Fuso, UD Trucks) running JIS N150/N200 batteries, and European trucks (Mercedes-Benz, Volvo, Scania, MAN, DAF, Iveco) that in this market are also commonly specified in the N150/N200 group, with DIN/EN codes as the European alternative. The dominant battery groups are N150, N200 and N100, and the operating environment — summer maximums of 40&ndash;50&deg;C — is the single largest stress on service life. Imports clear through SASO/SABER conformity and the GCC G-Mark, with Jeddah Islamic Port and King Abdulaziz Port Dammam as the main entry points.</DirectAnswer>

    <h2>Market overview</h2>
    <p>
      Saudi Arabia runs one of the largest heavy-duty truck fleets in the Gulf. Market research
      puts the Kingdom's heavy-duty truck market at roughly <strong>USD 9.6&nbsp;billion (2026)</strong>,
      with the major nameplates — Daimler Truck (Mercedes-Benz), Volvo Trucks, MAN, Isuzu and Hino —
      all active
      (<a href="https://www.mordorintelligence.com/industry-reports/saudi-arabia-heavy-duty-truck-market" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mordor Intelligence</a>).
      Japanese brands have deep, decades-long distribution: Hino has exported to the Kingdom since
      the 1970s through its Jamjoom Vehicles &amp; Equipment network
      (<a href="https://www.hino.com.sa/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hino Saudi Arabia</a>),
      and Isuzu has been represented by Al Yemni Group since 1980
      (<a href="https://isuzu-intl.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Isuzu International</a>).
    </p>
    <p>
      The precise split of the <em>installed</em> fleet by brand is not published in a single
      official source, so treat the following as the general structure rather than an exact
      proportion: a strong Japanese light-to-heavy truck base (Hino, Isuzu, Mitsubishi Fuso, UD
      Trucks) alongside a significant European long-haul segment (Mercedes-Benz, Volvo, Scania,
      MAN, DAF, Iveco). <em>Precise brand share: Not publicly verified.</em>
    </p>

    <h2>Climate &amp; duty demands</h2>
    <p>
      Heat is the defining stress. Riyadh's mean daily maximum sits at 42.5&deg;C in June and
      43.5&ndash;43.6&deg;C through July and August, with a recorded high of 49.8&deg;C
      (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Climate_of_Saudi_Arabia" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia — Climate of Saudi Arabia</a>;
      official data via the <a href="https://www.ncm.gov.sa/en/the-climate/climate-of-kingdom" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Center for Meteorology</a>).
      Summer maxima across the Kingdom typically run 35&ndash;45&deg;C, with occasional 50&deg;C in the
      eastern province.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a lead-acid starting battery this is harder duty than cold. Elevated temperature
      accelerates plate ageing and increases electrolyte water loss in flooded designs, which
      shortens service life and raises self-discharge while a vehicle sits. Industry references
      describe cycle life falling roughly 20% per 10&deg;C rise in ambient temperature, dropping
      below ~200 cycles at 40&deg;C
      (<a href="https://www.everexceed.com/blog/the-impact-of-high-temperatures-on-lead-acid-batteries-and-recommended-solutions_b663" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EverExceed</a>;
      <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0378775303009340" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Journal of Power Sources — lead-acid ageing</a>).
      The practical consequence for buyers: thick-plate flooded construction, correct electrolyte
      maintenance, or a sealed AGM option where the fleet prefers maintenance-free operation.
    </p>

    <h2>Vehicle mix &amp; common battery groups</h2>
    <p>
      The battery group follows the vehicle. On the Japanese side, Hino 700, Isuzu Giga, Fuso Super
      Great and UD Quon are specified in the JIS <strong>N150</strong> (and N200 where heavier
      cranking or a taller case is required), with N100 covering lighter trucks and buses. On the
      European side, Mercedes-Benz Actros/Atego, Volvo FH/FM, Scania R, MAN TGS/TGX, DAF XF and
      Iveco Stralis are also commonly listed in the N150/N200 group in Middle East fitment
      references, with DIN88/DIN100 as the European code alternative. The three JIS groups that
      dominate Saudi fleets are therefore:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Battery group</th><th>Dingwei reference</th><th>Capacity / CCA</th><th>Typical vehicle class</th></tr>
      </thead>
